      m","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_047132ab2882b2af016a75d95eedb481919f2fd49536b5d405","
      role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"{\"summary\":\"Product brief —
      Native msw menu-bar monitor\\n\\nProduct principle: ship a monitor and launcher, not a
      graphical replacement for the CLI. The repository defines three fixed, isolated workspaces
      (dev, playgrounds, personal), each with its own VM, persistent workspace and Docker volumes,
      loopback IP, browser hostname, resource limits, and GitHub trust boundary. The app should make
      their state and frequent safe actions instantly visible while leaving credential, destructive,
      and parameter-heavy workflows in the terminal.\\n\\nFirst-release experience: the menu-bar
      icon communicates aggregate state (all stopped, one or more running, action pending, or
      attention required). Its popover contains one row per workspace with name,
      running/stopped/error state, a compact CPU/memory indicator when available, and primary
      actions: Open Terminal, Open in Zed, Open Website, Start, Stop, and Restart. It also provides
      Refresh, Start All, Stop All with confirmation, Open Details, and Quit. Actions must show
      pending state, reject duplicates, refresh immediately on completion, and display an actionable
      error with the equivalent msw command.\\n\\nCapability matrix:\\nP0/menu bar — aggregate and
      per-workspace state; start/stop/restart for one workspace; start/stop all; launch the
      workspace shell; open Zed; open a configured or recent published port; manual refresh;
      pending/error/stale indicators. These map to msw status, start, stop, restart,
      dev/playgrounds/personal, zed, open, and url.\\nP0/detail window — one page per workspace
      showing state, fixed hostname, configured CPU/memory allocation and ceiling, live CPU/memory
      metrics while the page is visible, recent action/error, and explicit lifecycle/launch
      controls. Include the published-port reference and copyable URL builder. Do not auto-run msw
      disk because that command calls ensure_running and would wake a stopped VM.\\nP1/detail window
      — on-demand disk and Docker usage; repository inventory with Open Terminal/Open Zed actions;
      GitHub credential presence without revealing tokens; bounded log preview; quick health check;
      favorite ports and recent projects; backup destination, last successful backup, and a
      foreground backup job with progress and completion notification. These map to disk, repos,
      github status, logs, check, ports, and backup. Advanced arguments remain available through a
      “Copy command” or “Open in Terminal” affordance.\\nP1/menu bar — optional sustained CPU/memory
      pressure badges, unexpected-stop and action-failure notifications, configurable favorite
      website shortcuts, and a concise last-backup status.\\nP2/detail window — rolling resource
      history, sustained disk alerts, managed temporary tunnels, repository fetch/pull status,
      update availability, and a command/activity history. Clone or pull may be reconsidered only if
      a safe typed workflow proves materially faster than opening a terminal.\\nP2/platform —
      discover workspaces dynamically instead of assuming the current dev/playgrounds/personal
      array, support third-party detail panels through versioned capabilities, and expose automation
      through App Intents.\\nTerminal-only — exec; clone and all repository mutations; pull; push,
      including --yes and --force-with-lease; identity; GitHub setup/verify/remove; restore; resize;
      clean and --volumes; guest upgrade; MicroSandbox update; deep check; host repair; arbitrary
      log options; installer repair modes; docs/help/version. Push must remain terminal-only because
      the repository deliberately requires review of repository, branch, commit, and commits before
      the explicit host-only push path; restore replaces managed state and leaves every VM stopped;
      cleanup can delete unused Docker volumes; GitHub operations mutate Keychain-held credentials
      and can quarantine a workspace. Tunnels remain terminal-only in P0/P1 because their lifetime
      is tied to a foreground SSH process.\\n\\nUser stories:\\n1. As the owner, I can glance at one
      icon and know whether any workspace is running or needs attention.\\n2. I can start a stopped
      workspace and enter it in Ghostty or Zed without remembering a command; these launch paths may
      auto-start exactly as the CLI does.\\n3. I can open port 3000 in any workspace without
      confusing hosts, because each uses its documented dev.msw.test, playgrounds.msw.test, or
      personal.msw.test address.\\n4. I can stop an idle workspace or restart a failed one and see
      the confirmed resulting state.\\n5. I can inspect live resource pressure without keeping msw
      metrics --watch in a terminal.\\n6. I can diagnose an error from a bounded log excerpt and
      copy the exact terminal command for deeper work.\\n7. I can see whether guest-read and
      host-write GitHub credentials are present without the app retrieving or displaying either
      token.\\n8. I can run and track a cold backup later without being surprised that previously
      running VMs are temporarily stopped and then restarted.\\n\\nSuccess criteria: workspace state
      appears within two seconds of opening the popover and converges within ten seconds while idle;
      explicit actions refresh immediately and never execute twice concurrently; background polling
      never starts a stopped workspace; metrics collection runs only for visible/running workspaces
      and averages below 0.5% host CPU during idle use; every failure preserves stderr, names the
      affected workspace, and offers a valid CLI fallback; no credential value, command environment,
      or sensitive backup content is stored or logged; all controls are keyboard accessible and
      expose VoiceOver labels beyond color; Stop All, Restart All, backup, and any future
      state-changing bulk action require clear confirmation; notifications are deduplicated and
      pressure alerts require a sustained threshold with hysteresis; every documented command is
      deliberately classified as menu, detail, or terminal-only.\\n\\nSettings: launch at login;
      refresh interval with a conservative 10-second default and backoff on repeated failures;
      show/hide resource badges; per-workspace default port, scheme, favorite ports, and recent
      project paths; optional notifications for unexpected stops, failed actions, sustained
      CPU/memory/disk pressure, and long-running job completion; configurable thresholds and quiet
      hours; preferred terminal and editor detection; confirmation toggles only for reversible
      individual lifecycle actions, never for bulk or destructive actions. An optional global
      shortcut may toggle the popover but should be disabled by default; menu-local shortcuts can
      use Command-1/2/3 to select workspaces and Return to open the default action. No destructive
      action should receive a global shortcut.\\n\\nPersistence: use UserDefaults for preferences
      and favorites, plus a small atomic Application Support cache for recent ports/projects, last
      successful backup, and bounded metric samples. Runtime state remains authoritative in
      MicroSandbox and cached state must be visibly marked stale. Reuse the existing macOS Keychain
      boundary indirectly through msw; never copy, persist, or synchronize GitHub tokens. Avoid a
      database in P0.\\n\\nNotifications: default to action failures and unexpected transitions
      only. User-initiated start/stop success should stay silent; backup completion may notify
      because it is long-running. Threshold alerts should require several consecutive samples, issue
      one notification per incident, and clear only after recovery. Notification text should avoid
      tokens, raw logs, and sensitive repository paths.\\n\\nImplementation prerequisite and
      extensibility: do not scrape the current human-oriented msw status output, which combines msb
      ps with port prose, or keep parsing JSON with shell substring matching as sandbox_running
      currently does. Add a versioned, read-only machine contract before the native UI: status
      --json, metrics --json --once, disk --json with an explicit no-start mode, github status
      --json, and capabilities --json. The app should invoke the executable directly with argument
      arrays, never through a shell, and use a typed command adapter whose metadata declares
      read-only/state-changing/destructive, confirmation policy, timeout, and whether a stopped VM
      may be started. A cancellable job layer can later support backup, checks, and tunnels without
      expanding the popover.\\n\\nRoadmap: Phase 0 defines and tests the versioned JSON/read-only
      contract and action-safety metadata. Phase 1 ships the P0 menu bar and compact detail window,
      lifecycle serialization, launch actions, stale/error handling, accessibility, login setting,
      and direct CLI fallbacks. Phase 2 adds P1 diagnostics, repository inventory, GitHub presence,
      bounded logs, favorites, notifications, and supervised backup. Phase 3 adds opt-in history,
      managed tunnels, update awareness, App Intents, dynamic workspace discovery, and an extension
      registry; repository and credential mutation remain excluded unless separately
      threat-modeled.\\n\\nRepository grounding and verification: reviewed README.md,
      docs/MSW-CHEATSHEET.md, docs/SETUP-GUIDE.md, docs/TEST-REPORT.md, config.sh, and the complete
      command dispatch and relevant implementations in bin/msw. Cross-checked lifecycle auto-start
      behavior, fixed hosts and published ports, resource ceilings, cold-backup restart semantics,
      restore behavior, Keychain isolation, quarantine handling, and the documented 46-scenario
      release coverage. No project-wide tests, formatters, or linters were run, as requested. The
      repository was left untouched; existing unrelated working-tree changes were not
